<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">ascending order</h1>

<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<br class="ltx_break">
<p class="ltx_p">A <a class="nnexus_concept" href="http://mathworld.wolfram.com/Sequence.html">sequence</a> or arbitrary <a class="nnexus_concept" href="http://mathworld.wolfram.com/OrderedSet.html">ordered set</a> or one-dimensional array of numbers, <math id="p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline"><mi>a</mi></math>, is said to be in <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"><a class="nnexus_concepts" href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="this.nextSibling.style.display='inline'">ascending order</a><sup style="display: none;"><a class="nnexus_concept" href="http://planetmath.org/ascendingorder"><img src="http://planetmath.org/sites/default/files/fab-favicon.ico" alt="Planetmath"></img></a><a class="nnexus_concept" href="http://planetmath.org/polynomial"><img src="http://planetmath.org/sites/default/files/fab-favicon.ico" alt="Planetmath"></img></a></sup></span> if each <math id="p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}\leq a_{i+1}" display="inline"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. For example, the <a class="nnexus_concepts" href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="this.nextSibling.style.display='inline'">Fibonacci sequence</a><sup style="display: none;"><a class="nnexus_concept" href="http://mathworld.wolfram.com/FibonacciSequence.html"><img src="http://mathworld.wolfram.com/favicon_mathworld.png" alt="Mathworld"></img></a><a class="nnexus_concept" href="http://planetmath.org/fibonaccisequence"><img src="http://planetmath.org/sites/default/files/fab-favicon.ico" alt="Planetmath"></img></a></sup> is in ascending order: 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21 … The <a class="nnexus_concepts" href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="this.nextSibling.style.display='inline'">Perrin sequence</a><sup style="display: none;"><a class="nnexus_concept" href="http://mathworld.wolfram.com/PerrinSequence.html"><img src="http://mathworld.wolfram.com/favicon_mathworld.png" alt="Mathworld"></img></a><a class="nnexus_concept" href="http://planetmath.org/perrinsequence"><img src="http://planetmath.org/sites/default/files/fab-favicon.ico" alt="Planetmath"></img></a></sup> is not in ascending order: 3, 0, 2, 3, 2, 5, 5, 7, 10, 12, 17 …</p>
</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para">
<p class="ltx_p">In a trivial sense, the sequence of values of the sign function is in ascending order: … -1, -1, -1, 0, 1, 1, 1… When each <math id="p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}&lt;a_{i+1}" display="inline"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> in the sequence, set or array, then it can be said to be in <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">strictly ascending order</span>.
</p>
